ghtly faster.\n\nAlso, the binary search insertion uses KNNEntry struct with dist and label; that's fine.\n\nAnother transformation: Use local variables for to_evaluate.x and .y to avoid repeated field access inside loop? Already done implicitly.\n\nMaybe we can combine all groups into a single flat array of points with labels stored separately or as part of point. But then we need to know group boundaries for frequency counting later? Actually the final histogram counts labels from top-k entries, not from original groups; so we don't need group info after distance computation except label per point.\n\nWe could store all points in a single array of structs (PointWithLabel) and iterate over them. That would improve memory locality because all data is contiguous. However, parsing currently reads each group separately; we can still allocate one big array for all points after reading n_groups? But we don't know total number of points until we parse all groups. We could first count total points by scanning input twice? Not allowed.\n\nAlternative: During parsing, store points in a dynamic array (realloc) that grows as we read each group. That would create one contiguous block for all points. Then distance computation loop becomes a single flat loop over all points, which is cache-friendly and allows prefetching. The label per point can be stored alongside coordinates.\n\nBut the current code already processes groups sequentially; within a group, xs/ys are contiguous but separate arrays. A flat array of structs would have better spatial locality because each point's x,y,label are together.\n\nLet's propose that: Change data layout to store all points in a single dynamic array of PointWithLabel (x,y,label). During parsing, we append points from each group. Then knn function receives this flat array and total number of points. This eliminates the Group struct entirely for computation. The distance loop becomes:\n\nfor i = 0; i < n_points; i++:\n    float d = euclidean_dist